# expect: parse ok
# description: large CI/CD pipeline with heavy orchestration, parallel groups, code blocks, verify, memory, execution state, agent_context
agm: 1.0
package: cicd.pipeline.enterprise
version: 1.8.0
title: Enterprise CI/CD Pipeline Architecture
owner: devops-platform
description:
  Multi-stage CI/CD pipeline for a polyglot microservices architecture.
  Covers build, test, security scan, artifact publishing, staging
  deployment, integration testing, production canary, and rollback
  procedures.
status: active
default_load: executable
tags: [cicd, devops, kubernetes, security, automation]
target_runtime: octopus
load_profiles:
  minimal:
    filter: "priority in [critical] AND type in [decision]"
    estimated_tokens: 900
  operational:
    filter: "priority in [critical, high]"
    estimated_tokens: 5800
  executable:
    filter: "code is present OR type = orchestration"
    estimated_tokens: 7500
  full:
    filter: "*"
    estimated_tokens: 12000

node ci.toolchain
type: facts
stability: high
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [toolchain, infrastructure]
summary: rust 1.80 toolchain; docker buildx multi-arch; trivy vulnerability scanner; helm 3.14; argocd gitops; azure devops agents
items:
  - compiler=rustc_1.80_stable
  - build_tool=cargo
  - container_builder=docker_buildx
  - vulnerability_scanner=trivy_0.52
  - secret_scanner=gitleaks_8
  - sast_tool=clippy_semgrep
  - helm_version=3.14
  - gitops=argocd_2.11
  - registry=harbor_2.10
  - ci_platform=azure_devops
  - test_runner=cargo_nextest
  - coverage_tool=cargo_llvm_cov
detail:
  The toolchain is pinned via rust-toolchain.toml and renovate bot
  manages dependency updates with auto-merge for patch versions.
  Docker buildx produces linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 images in a
  single build step. Trivy scans both container images and filesystem
  for CVEs. ArgoCD handles gitops-based deployment to all environments.

node ci.branch.rules
type: rules
stability: high
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [branching, policy]
summary: trunk-based development with short-lived feature branches, squash merge, and mandatory ci checks
items:
  - main_branch_is_always_deployable
  - feature_branches_must_be_under_3_days_old
  - squash_merge_required_for_all_prs
  - minimum_2_approvals_for_production_changes
  - minimum_1_approval_for_non_production_changes
  - ci_must_pass_before_merge
  - no_force_push_to_main
  - branch_names_follow_type_slash_ticket_dash_description
  - release_tags_follow_semver_v_prefix
  - hotfix_branches_merge_to_main_and_backport
detail:
  Trunk-based development minimizes merge conflicts and integration
  pain. Feature branches should be as small as possible. Long-running
  branches are reviewed weekly and either merged or closed. Branch
  protection rules are enforced at the repository level.

node ci.build.rust
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [build, rust]
depends: [ci.toolchain, ci.branch.rules]
input: [source_code, cargo_toml, rust_toolchain_toml]
output: [compiled_binaries, build_artifacts, build_logs]
summary: compile rust workspace with incremental caching, dependency audit, and reproducible builds
steps:
  - restore cargo registry and target directory caches
  - run cargo fetch to download dependencies
  - run cargo audit to check for known vulnerabilities
  - run cargo fmt --check to verify formatting
  - run cargo clippy -D warnings for lint checks
  - run cargo build --release --locked for reproducible builds
  - save target directory cache keyed by lockfile hash
  - upload build artifacts to pipeline storage
code:
  lang: yaml
  target: azure-pipelines/stages/build.yaml
  action: create
  body: |
    stages:
      - stage: Build
        displayName: 'Build & Lint'
        jobs:
          - job: RustBuild
            pool:
              vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'
            steps:
              - task: Cache@2
                inputs:
                  key: 'cargo | "$(Agent.OS)" | Cargo.lock'
                  path: $(CARGO_HOME)/registry
                displayName: 'Cache cargo registry'
              - script: |
                  rustup show
                  cargo fetch
                  cargo audit --deny warnings
                  cargo fmt --check
                  cargo clippy -- -D warnings
                  cargo build --release --locked
                displayName: 'Build workspace'
              - publish: target/release
                artifact: binaries
                displayName: 'Publish binaries'
verify:
  - type: file_exists
    file: azure-pipelines/stages/build.yaml
  - type: file_contains
    file: azure-pipelines/stages/build.yaml
    pattern: "cargo build --release --locked"
  - type: command
    run: cargo build --release --locked
    expect: exit_code_0
agent_context:
  load_nodes: [ci.toolchain]
  load_files:
    - path: Cargo.toml
      range: full
    - path: Cargo.lock
      range: [1, 20]
    - path: rust-toolchain.toml
      range: full
  system_hint: "Azure DevOps pipeline YAML. Use task references not inline scripts where possible."
  max_tokens: 4000
execution_status: pending

node ci.test.unit
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [testing, unit-tests]
depends: [ci.build.rust]
input: [compiled_workspace]
output: [test_results_xml, coverage_report]
summary: run unit tests with nextest for parallel execution and llvm-cov for coverage reporting
steps:
  - restore build cache from previous stage
  - run cargo nextest run with junit output
  - run cargo llvm-cov for coverage
  - fail if coverage below 80 percent threshold
  - publish test results and coverage to pipeline
code:
  lang: yaml
  target: azure-pipelines/stages/test.yaml
  action: create
  body: |
    stages:
      - stage: Test
        displayName: 'Unit Tests & Coverage'
        dependsOn: Build
        jobs:
          - job: UnitTests
            pool:
              vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'
            steps:
              - download: current
                artifact: binaries
              - script: |
                  cargo nextest run --profile ci --message-format libtest-json > results.json
                  cargo llvm-cov --lcov --output-path lcov.info
                  COVERAGE=$(cargo llvm-cov --summary-only | grep -oP '\d+\.\d+%' | head -1)
                  echo "Coverage: $COVERAGE"
                displayName: 'Run tests with coverage'
              - task: PublishTestResults@2
                inputs:
                  testResultsFormat: 'JUnit'
                  testResultsFiles: 'results.xml'
verify:
  - type: file_exists
    file: azure-pipelines/stages/test.yaml
  - type: command
    run: cargo nextest run --profile ci
    expect: exit_code_0
execution_status: pending
memory:
  - action: upsert
    topic: ci.metrics
    key: ci.test.last.coverage
    value: pending measurement
    scope: session

node ci.test.integration
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [testing, integration-tests]
depends: [ci.test.unit]
input: [compiled_workspace, docker_compose_file]
output: [integration_test_results]
summary: run integration tests against real database and message broker using docker compose test environment
steps:
  - start docker compose with postgres, redis, and kafka
  - wait for all services to be healthy
  - run database migrations
  - seed test data
  - run integration test suite with nextest
  - collect test results
  - tear down docker compose environment
code:
  lang: bash
  target: scripts/integration-test.sh
  action: create
  body: |
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail

    echo "Starting test infrastructure..."
    docker compose -f docker-compose.test.yaml up -d --wait

    echo "Running migrations..."
    cargo run -p migration-tool -- migrate

    echo "Running integration tests..."
    cargo nextest run --profile integration \
      --test-threads=4 \
      --retries=2

    EXIT_CODE=$?

    echo "Tearing down..."
    docker compose -f docker-compose.test.yaml down -v

    exit $EXIT_CODE
verify:
  - type: file_exists
    file: scripts/integration-test.sh
  - type: file_exists
    file: docker-compose.test.yaml
  - type: command
    run: bash scripts/integration-test.sh
    expect: exit_code_0
execution_status: pending

node ci.security.scan
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [security, scanning, compliance]
depends: [ci.build.rust]
related_to: [ci.test.unit]
input: [source_code, container_image]
output: [vulnerability_report, secret_scan_report, sast_report]
summary: comprehensive security scanning with trivy for cves, gitleaks for secrets, and semgrep for sast patterns
steps:
  - run gitleaks detect on full git history
  - run trivy filesystem scan on source directory
  - build container image for scanning
  - run trivy image scan with severity HIGH and CRITICAL
  - run semgrep with rust and owasp rulesets
  - aggregate results into unified security report
  - fail pipeline if any CRITICAL findings
  - upload report to security dashboard
code_blocks:
  - lang: yaml
    target: azure-pipelines/stages/security.yaml
    action: create
    body: |
      stages:
        - stage: SecurityScan
          displayName: 'Security Scanning'
          dependsOn: Build
          jobs:
            - job: SecretScan
              pool:
                vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'
              steps:
                - script: gitleaks detect --source . --verbose
                  displayName: 'Secret scanning'
            - job: VulnScan
              pool:
                vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'
              steps:
                - script: |
                    trivy fs --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 .
                    trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 $(IMAGE_TAG)
                  displayName: 'Vulnerability scanning'
            - job: SAST
              pool:
                vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'
              steps:
                - script: semgrep --config auto --config p/rust --config p/owasp-top-ten --error
                  displayName: 'SAST analysis'
  - lang: toml
    target: trivy.yaml
    action: create
    body: |
      severity:
        - HIGH
        - CRITICAL
      ignore-unfixed: true
      skip-dirs:
        - target
        - .git
        - node_modules
      timeout: 300s
verify:
  - type: file_exists
    file: azure-pipelines/stages/security.yaml
  - type: file_contains
    file: azure-pipelines/stages/security.yaml
    pattern: "gitleaks detect"
  - type: file_contains
    file: azure-pipelines/stages/security.yaml
    pattern: "trivy"
agent_context:
  load_nodes: [ci.toolchain]
  load_files:
    - path: .trivyignore
      range: full
    - path: .semgrep.yaml
      range: full
  system_hint: "Security scans must not be skippable. CRITICAL findings block the pipeline unconditionally."
execution_status: pending

node ci.container.build
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [container, docker, registry]
depends: [ci.build.rust, ci.security.scan]
input: [compiled_binaries, dockerfile]
output: [container_image_digest, sbom]
summary: multi-stage docker build with distroless base, sbom generation, and harbor registry push
steps:
  - copy release binaries into build context
  - build multi-arch image with docker buildx
  - generate sbom with syft
  - sign image with cosign
  - push to harbor registry with semver and sha tags
  - run trivy scan on final image
code:
  lang: dockerfile
  target: Dockerfile
  action: create
  body: |
    FROM rust:1.80-slim AS builder
    WORKDIR /app
    COPY Cargo.toml Cargo.lock ./
    COPY crates/ ./crates/
    RUN cargo build --release --locked

    FROM gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12:nonroot
    COPY --from=builder /app/target/release/api-server /usr/local/bin/
    EXPOSE 8080
    ENTRYPOINT ["api-server"]
verify:
  - type: file_exists
    file: Dockerfile
  - type: file_contains
    file: Dockerfile
    pattern: "distroless"
  - type: command
    run: docker build --check .
    expect: exit_code_0
execution_status: pending

node ci.deploy.staging
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [deployment, staging, argocd]
depends: [ci.container.build, ci.test.integration]
input: [container_image_digest, helm_chart]
output: [staging_deployment_status, staging_url]
summary: deploy to staging via argocd sync with automated smoke tests and environment-specific config
steps:
  - update helm values with new image digest
  - commit values change to gitops repository
  - trigger argocd sync for staging environment
  - wait for rollout to complete
  - run smoke test suite against staging url
  - verify health endpoints respond 200
  - run lighthouse performance audit on staging
code:
  lang: bash
  target: scripts/deploy-staging.sh
  action: create
  body: |
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail

    IMAGE_DIGEST="${1:?Usage: deploy-staging.sh <image-digest>}"
    STAGING_URL="https://staging.platform.internal"

    echo "Updating staging values..."
    yq e ".image.digest = \"$IMAGE_DIGEST\"" -i deploy/environments/staging/values.yaml

    echo "Committing to gitops repo..."
    cd /tmp/gitops-repo
    git add .
    git commit -m "chore(staging): update image to $IMAGE_DIGEST"
    git push origin main

    echo "Syncing ArgoCD..."
    argocd app sync platform-staging --prune --timeout 300

    echo "Running smoke tests..."
    curl -sf "$STAGING_URL/health" || exit 1
    cargo nextest run --profile smoke -- --test-threads=1
verify:
  - type: file_exists
    file: scripts/deploy-staging.sh
  - type: command
    run: bash -n scripts/deploy-staging.sh
    expect: exit_code_0
execution_status: pending
memory:
  - action: upsert
    topic: deployment.tracking
    key: ci.staging.last.deploy
    value: pending
    scope: project
    ttl: permanent
  - action: upsert
    topic: deployment.tracking
    key: ci.staging.image.digest
    value: pending
    scope: session

node ci.deploy.production
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [deployment, production, canary]
depends: [ci.deploy.staging]
input: [staging_validation_passed, container_image_digest]
output: [production_deployment_status, canary_metrics]
summary: production canary deployment with progressive traffic shifting, metric gates, and automatic rollback
steps:
  - verify staging deployment passed all checks
  - update production helm values with image digest
  - deploy canary with 5 percent traffic
  - monitor error rate and latency for 10 minutes
  - if metrics healthy increase to 25 percent
  - monitor for additional 10 minutes
  - promote to 100 percent
  - update deployment record in tracking system
  - notify team in slack channel
execution_status: pending
memory:
  - action: upsert
    topic: deployment.tracking
    key: ci.production.deploy.status
    value: initiated
    scope: project
    ttl: permanent

node ci.rollback.procedure
type: exception
stability: high
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [rollback, incident-response]
depends: [ci.deploy.production]
summary: automatic and manual rollback procedures when canary metrics breach thresholds or hotfix is needed
detail:
  Rollback can be triggered automatically by Flagger when canary
  analysis fails, or manually by any on-call engineer through
  the ArgoCD dashboard or CLI. The rollback restores the previous
  known-good image digest and triggers a fresh deployment cycle.
resolution:
  - flagger auto-rollback on metric threshold breach
  - manual rollback via argocd app rollback command
  - hotfix branch from last known good tag
  - incident channel created in slack for communication
  - post-incident review within 48 hours
  - document root cause in incident database

node ci.dependency.audit.glossary
type: glossary
stability: high
priority: normal
confidence: high
tags: [glossary, security]
summary: terminology for dependency management and vulnerability tracking in the ci pipeline
items:
  - cve: common vulnerabilities and exposures, a cataloged security vulnerability
  - sbom: software bill of materials, a list of all dependencies in a build artifact
  - sast: static application security testing, analyzing source code for vulnerabilities
  - dast: dynamic application security testing, testing running applications for vulnerabilities
  - supply_chain_attack: compromise of a software dependency to inject malicious code
  - advisory: security notice from a package registry about a known vulnerability
  - remediation: process of fixing or mitigating a known vulnerability

node ci.skip.tests.antipattern
type: anti_pattern
stability: high
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [anti-pattern, testing, culture]
conflicts: [ci.branch.rules]
summary: skipping ci tests or using no-verify flags to speed up merges undermines pipeline reliability
detail:
  Developers under deadline pressure sometimes bypass CI checks using
  git push --no-verify, pipeline skip comments, or draft-to-ready PR
  tricks. This erodes trust in the pipeline and allows regressions to
  reach production. Every pipeline bypass in the last year correlated
  with a subsequent incident.

node ci.monorepo.decision
type: decision
stability: high
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [architecture, monorepo]
depends: [ci.toolchain, ci.branch.rules]
see_also: [ci.build.rust]
summary: cargo workspace monorepo with per-crate ci triggers instead of polyrepo with separate pipelines
rationale:
  - atomic cross-crate refactoring
  - shared ci configuration and toolchain pinning
  - single code review for cross-cutting changes
  - cargo workspace handles dependency deduplication
tradeoffs:
  - ci must detect affected crates to avoid full rebuilds
  - repository size grows faster than polyrepo
  - access control is coarser than per-repo permissions
  - git clone time increases over time

node ci.pipeline.orchestration
type: orchestration
stability: medium
priority: critical
confidence: high
summary: end-to-end ci/cd pipeline orchestration from build through production deployment
parallel_groups:
  - group: 1-build
    nodes: [ci.build.rust]
    strategy: sequential
  - group: 2-quality-gates
    nodes: [ci.test.unit, ci.security.scan]
    strategy: parallel
    requires: [1-build]
    max_concurrency: 3
  - group: 3-integration
    nodes: [ci.test.integration, ci.container.build]
    strategy: parallel
    requires: [2-quality-gates]
    max_concurrency: 2
  - group: 4-staging
    nodes: [ci.deploy.staging]
    strategy: sequential
    requires: [3-integration]
  - group: 5-production
    nodes: [ci.deploy.production]
    strategy: sequential
    requires: [4-staging]
detail:
  The pipeline flows strictly left-to-right through five phases.
  Build is the foundation. Quality gates (unit tests and security
  scans) run in parallel. Integration tests and container build
  run in parallel after quality gates pass. Staging deployment
  requires all integration work. Production deployment requires
  successful staging validation. Any failure in groups 1-4 blocks
  production deployment entirely.
memory:
  - action: upsert
    topic: pipeline.metrics
    key: pipeline.last.full.run.duration
    value: measuring
    scope: session
  - action: upsert
    topic: pipeline.metrics
    key: pipeline.run.id
    value: pending
    scope: session
